   "html_text": "Some truths are hard to recognize as they are highly specific, and thus unlikely to hold. Recognizing that global temperature will rise may be easy, but recognizing that it will rise by 2.3 Degrees Celsius is hard. Whenever some choice alternative (and thus its correctness) is highly specific, competence in jury models is threatened. Suppose a climate panel must form a belief about the proposition ⟦M⟧ that global temperature will rise by 2.3 degrees. Someone’s competence given ⟦M⟧ (more generall",
   "md_text": "Some truths are hard to recognize as they are highly specific, and thus unlikely to hold. Recognizing that global temperature will rise may be easy, but recognizing that it will rise by 2.3 Degrees Celsius is hard. Whenever some choice alternative (and thus its correctness) is highly specific, competence in jury models is threatened. Suppose a climate panel must form a belief about the proposition ⟦M⟧ that global temperature will rise by 2.3 degrees. Someone’s competence given ⟦M⟧ (more generall"
  },
  {
   "i": 22,
   "shape_h": "p",
   "shape_m": "p",
   "html_text": "This section states and discusses three jury theorems about majority decisions between two alternatives. They assume a population of individuals, labelled ⟦M⟧ representing the possible members of the decision-making group. The population might contain, for example, all citizens of a state (for a political decision) or all scientists (for a scientific decision). It is infinite—an idealisation needed for considering arbitrarily large decision bodies. The group has any finite size ⟦M⟧ and consists ",
   "md_text": "This section states and discusses three jury theorems about majority decisions between two alternatives. They assume a population of individuals, labelled ⟦M⟧ representing the possible members of the decision-making group. The population might contain, for example, all citizens of a state (for a political decision) or all scientists (for a scientific decision). It is infinite—an idealisation needed for considering arbitrarily large decision bodies. The group has any finite size ⟦M⟧ and consists "
  },
  {
   "i": 9,
   "shape_h": "ul",
   "shape_m": "ul",
   "html_text": "the choice problem is binary, like the guilty-or-innocent problem of juries; the voting procedure is majority voting; the premises on individuals are competence and independence assumptions of particularly simple kinds; the conclusion is that “crowds are wise” in the twofold sense that the probability of a correct group decision (i) increases in group size and (ii) converges to one as group size approaches infinity.",
   "md_text": "the choice problem is binary, like the guilty-or-innocent problem of juries; the voting procedure is majority voting; the premises on individuals are competence and independence assumptions of particularly simple kinds; the conclusion is that “crowds are wise” in the twofold sense that the probability of a correct group decision (i) increases in group size and (ii) converges to one as group size approaches infinity."
  }
